### What is a custom watermark strategy?

A `WatermarkStrategy` in Flink is a factory for two collaborators: a `TimestampAssigner` that extracts event time from each record, and a `WatermarkGenerator` that decides when to advance the watermark. You implement a custom one when the built-in strategies do not fit your data.

```java
import org.apache.flink.api.common.eventtime.*;
import java.time.Duration;

// Built-in strategy (most common)
WatermarkStrategy<Transaction> builtIn =
    WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
            Duration.ofSeconds(5))
        .withTimestampAssigner(
            (txn, ts) -> txn.getEventTimeMillis());

// Custom strategy when built-in is not enough
WatermarkStrategy<Transaction> custom =
    new WatermarkStrategy<Transaction>() {
        @Override
        public TimestampAssigner<Transaction> createTimestampAssigner(
                TimestampAssignerSupplier.Context ctx) {
            return (txn, ts) -> txn.getEventTimeMillis();
        }

        @Override
        public WatermarkGenerator<Transaction> createWatermarkGenerator(
                WatermarkGeneratorSupplier.Context ctx) {
            return new CustomWatermarkGenerator();
        }
    };
```

### How does a periodic watermark generator work?

A periodic generator emits watermarks on a fixed interval controlled by `env.getConfig().setAutoWatermarkInterval(...)`. The `onEvent` callback tracks state, and `onPeriodicEmit` emits the watermark.

```java
public class BoundedOutOfOrderGenerator
        implements WatermarkGenerator<Transaction> {

    private long maxTimestamp = Long.MIN_VALUE;
    private final long maxOutOfOrdernessMillis;

    public BoundedOutOfOrderGenerator(long maxOutOfOrdernessMillis) {
        this.maxOutOfOrdernessMillis = maxOutOfOrdernessMillis;
    }

    @Override
    public void onEvent(Transaction txn, long eventTimestamp,
            WatermarkOutput output) {
        // Track the maximum event time seen
        maxTimestamp = Math.max(maxTimestamp, eventTimestamp);
    }

    @Override
    public void onPeriodicEmit(WatermarkOutput output) {
        // Emit watermark = max_seen - out_of_orderness
        output.emitWatermark(
            new Watermark(maxTimestamp - maxOutOfOrdernessMillis));
    }
}
```

```text
Auto watermark interval: 200ms (default)
onEvent: tracks maxTimestamp
onPeriodicEmit (every 200ms): emits Watermark(maxTimestamp - 5000)
```

### How does a punctuated watermark generator work?

A punctuated generator emits a watermark inside `onEvent` when it sees a specific marker record. This is useful when your source has explicit completeness markers like an end-of-batch record.

```java
public class PunctuatedGenerator
        implements WatermarkGenerator<Transaction> {

    @Override
    public void onEvent(Transaction txn, long eventTimestamp,
            WatermarkOutput output) {
        // Emit watermark when we see an end-of-batch marker
        if (txn.isEndOfBatch()) {
            output.emitWatermark(new Watermark(eventTimestamp));
        }
    }

    @Override
    public void onPeriodicEmit(WatermarkOutput output) {
        // No periodic emission -- watermarks are purely punctuated
    }
}
```

```text
Stream: T1 T2 T3 [END_BATCH ts=10:05] T4 T5 [END_BATCH ts=10:10]
                  ▲                         ▲
            Watermark(10:05)          Watermark(10:10)
```

### What happens when out-of-orderness is too high?

A large out-of-orderness delay means the watermark trails far behind the latest event time. Every event-time window waits that much longer before firing, increasing end-to-end latency and holding more open windows in memory.

```java
// Too high: 60 seconds of out-of-orderness
WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
    Duration.ofSeconds(60));
// Watermark = max_seen - 60s
// Every 1-minute window waits an extra 60s before firing
// 2x the windows open in memory at any time
// End-to-end latency increases by 60 seconds
```

### What happens when out-of-orderness is too low?

A zero or very small delay means the watermark hugs the latest event time. Any slightly-delayed event arrives after the watermark has passed its window and is treated as late, silently understating results.

```java
// Too low: 0 seconds (monotonous timestamps)
WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forMonotonousTimestamps();
// Watermark = max_seen - 0
// An event delayed by even 1 millisecond is dropped as late
// Settlement totals may undercount transactions
```

```text
Out-of-orderness too LOW (0s):
  Event at 10:00:30, arrives at 10:00:31
  Watermark already at 10:00:31 (from another event)
  10:00:30 < 10:00:31 → DROPPED! Total is wrong.

Out-of-orderness correct (5s):
  Event at 10:00:30, arrives at 10:00:31
  Watermark at 10:00:26 (max 10:00:31 - 5s)
  10:00:30 > 10:00:26 → ACCEPTED. Total is correct.
```

### How do you choose the right out-of-orderness?

Measure the actual lateness distribution of your source and set the out-of-orderness to cover roughly the 99th percentile of observed delay. Handle the rare tail with allowed lateness plus a side output.

```java
// Step 1: Measure your data's lateness distribution
// ingestion_time - event_time for each record
// P50 = 200ms, P95 = 2s, P99 = 5s, P99.9 = 30s

// Step 2: Set out-of-orderness to cover P99
WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
    Duration.ofSeconds(5));  // covers 99% of late events

// Step 3: Handle the 1% tail with allowed lateness + side output
stream.keyBy(txn -> txn.getAccountId())
    .window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(Time.minutes(1)))
    .allowedLateness(Time.seconds(30))  // covers P99.9
    .sideOutputLateData(lateTag);       // capture the rest
```

### How do you handle idle sources?

If a Kafka partition stops producing, its watermark stops advancing and holds back the global watermark, freezing all windows. Use `withIdleness` to mark idle sources so they do not block progress.

```java
WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
        Duration.ofSeconds(5))
    .withTimestampAssigner((txn, ts) -> txn.getEventTimeMillis())
    .withIdleness(Duration.ofMinutes(1));  // mark idle after 1 min
// If a partition produces nothing for 1 minute,
// it is excluded from watermark calculation
// so other partitions can still advance the global watermark
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is guessing the out-of-orderness instead of measuring it. Another mistake is forgetting `withIdleness`, causing a single idle partition to freeze all windows indefinitely.

```java
// WRONG -- arbitrary guess, too tight
WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
    Duration.ofMillis(100));  // Guess: most events arrive in 100ms
// Reality: mobile network delays cause 5s gaps → dropped events

// CORRECT -- measured from real data
WatermarkStrategy.<Transaction>forBoundedOutOfOrderness(
    Duration.ofSeconds(5));  // Measured: P99 lateness = 5s
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The professional approach is not to guess but to measure the real lateness distribution, set the out-of-orderness to the 99th percentile, and handle the tail with allowed lateness and a side output. Use punctuated watermarks when the source has explicit batch-completion markers for precise time advancement without delay-based guessing.
